import json
from pathlib import Path
p=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
items=[]
def add(record,kind,title,notes,statement,reconstruction='',cls='E',fate='',status='',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',match='exact',verification='read the result',development='annotated',problem='',refs=(),confidence='high',area='Representation theory',pages='',speaker=None):
    n=sum(i['record']==record for i in items)+1
    d=dict(id=f'1981-{record}.{n}',record=record,pages=pages,kind=kind,title=title,notes=notes,statement=statement,reconstruction=reconstruction,**{'class':cls},fate=fate,present_status='As of 11 November 2026: '+status,literal_correctness=correct,publication_match=match,verification=verification,development=development,problem=problem,worth='',refs=list(refs),confidence=confidence,area=area)
    if speaker:d['speaker']=speaker
    items.append(d)
def save():
    (p/'items.json').write_text(json.dumps(items,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
add(538,'definition','Triangular decomposition and simple-root generators',
 r'The section begins with \(\mathfrak g\) complex semisimple, its Cartan subalgebra, \(\dim\mathfrak h=n\), generators \(e_i,h_i,f_i\), and schematic brackets identifying \(\mathfrak n_+\) and a Borel subalgebra.',
 r'Fix a Cartan subalgebra \(\mathfrak h\), positive roots, and simple roots \(\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_n\). Then \(\mathfrak g=\mathfrak n_-\oplus\mathfrak h\oplus\mathfrak n_+\), \(\mathfrak b=\mathfrak h\oplus\mathfrak n_+\), \(h_i=\alpha_i^\vee\), and \([e_i,f_j]=\delta_{ij}h_i\). The \(e_i\) generate \(\mathfrak n_+\) as a Lie algebra; their linear span need not equal it.',
 r'The root system, positive system, and distinction between generating and spanning are supplied to make the schematic drawing precise.',
 fate=r'This is the classical setup recalled in \citet[\S1]{brylinski1981differential}; the 1981 article reports a lecture delivered in August--September 1980.',status='standard structure theory, not a new seminar claim.',refs=['brylinski1981differential'],pages='P000995')
add(538,'definition','Highest vectors and highest weights',
 r'A highest vector is first characterized by \(e_i v=0\); the notes observe that \(h_jv\) has the same property. Later they impose \(hv=\chi(h)v\).',
 r'A highest-weight vector of weight \(\chi\in\mathfrak h^*\) is a nonzero vector killed by \(\mathfrak n_+\) and simultaneously satisfying \(hv=\chi(h)v\) for every \(h\in\mathfrak h\). The common kernel of the \(e_i\) is \(\mathfrak h\)-stable because \([e_i,h_j]\) is a scalar multiple of \(e_i\). In any nonzero finite-dimensional \(\mathfrak g\)-module this kernel contains a common \(\mathfrak h\)-eigenvector.',
 r'Being killed by the raising operators alone does not select an eigenvector in an arbitrary module. Finite dimension, or the weight-module hypothesis, is needed for the stated extraction of a highest weight.',
 fate=r'The standard highest-weight construction is recalled in \citet[\S1]{brylinski1981differential}. The kernel calculation here explains the notes\textquotesingle\ omitted transition.',status='correct after distinguishing a singular vector from a chosen highest-weight eigenvector.',refs=['brylinski1981differential'],pages='P000995')
add(538,'construction','Verma module and its unique simple quotient',
 r'The notes introduce a universal representation \(M(\chi)\) with highest vector of weight \(\chi\), and its unique irreducible quotient \(L(\chi)\).',
 r'Let \(\mathbb C_\chi\) be the one-dimensional \(\mathfrak b\)-module on which \(\mathfrak n_+\) acts by zero and \(h\) by \(\chi(h)\). Then \(M(\chi)=U(\mathfrak g)\otimes_{U(\mathfrak b)}\mathbb C_\chi\). It is generated by \(v_\chi\), and every module generated by a highest vector of this weight is a quotient. Its sum of proper submodules is proper, since none meets the one-dimensional top weight space, and hence it has a unique simple quotient \(L(\chi)\).',
 r'The induction formula and the explanation of uniqueness are explicit reconstructions of the compressed universal-property statement.',
 fate=r'Already part of highest-weight theory before the talk; see \citet[\S1]{brylinski1981differential} and the category construction in \citet[\S1]{bernsteingelfandgelfand1976category}.',status='established for every complex weight; finite-dimensionality of the simple quotient requires dominant integrality.',refs=['brylinski1981differential','bernsteingelfandgelfand1976category'],pages='P000995')
add(538,'claim','Finite length versus arbitrary subquotients',
 r'The notes say that \(M(\chi)\) has a finite composition series and that every subquotient has the form \(L(\psi)\).',
 r'For finite-dimensional complex semisimple \(\mathfrak g\), every Verma module has finite Jordan--Hölder length, and every simple composition factor is a highest-weight module \(L(\psi)\). An arbitrary subquotient need not be simple, and therefore need not be isomorphic to any \(L(\psi)\).',
 r'The necessary emendation is ``simple subquotient\textquotesingle\ or ``composition factor\textquotesingle. It is a correction to the mathematical wording, not an alleged mistranscription.',
 fate=r'Finite length is recalled explicitly in \citet[Introduction]{gabberjoseph1981towards}. The article appeared in 1981, and the result was standard before the seminar.',status='the finite-length claim is established; the unrestricted subquotient clause is false.',correct='false as written',refs=['gabberjoseph1981towards'],pages='P000995')
add(538,'problem','Composition multiplicities in a Verma module',
 r'The first question asks for the multiplicity of \(M(\psi)\) in a composition series of \(M(\chi)\), but the next displayed definition uses \(L(\psi)\). Both the Russian and the scan contain this change of letter.',
 r'Determine \(m(\chi,\psi)=[M(\chi):L(\psi)]\), the Jordan--Hölder multiplicity of the simple module \(L(\psi)\) in \(M(\chi)\). A reducible Verma module cannot itself be a composition factor.',
 r'The immediately following formula fixes the intended question unambiguously: the earlier \(M(\psi)\) must be emended to \(L(\psi)\).',
 fate=r'This is a recurrence of GP-1979-41. The multiplicities in a regular integral block were determined by the Kazhdan--Lusztig theorem, whose proof was announced in 1980 and published in 1981; \citet[Introduction and \S1]{brylinski1981differential} records the proof context.',status='settled in the finite-dimensional semisimple setting; the source slip creates no separate problem.',correct='false as written',problem='GP-1979-41',refs=['brylinski1981differential'],pages='P000995')
add(538,'theorem','Central-character linkage and the rho shift',
 r'Nonzero multiplicity is said to require that \(\chi+\rho\) and \(\psi+\rho\) lie in one Weyl orbit; \(\rho(h_i)=1\). The subsequent definition of dominant integral weight changes the letter \(\chi\) to \(\lambda\).',
 r'Write \(w\cdot\chi=w(\chi+\rho)-\rho\), where \(\rho\) is half the sum of positive roots. The center of \(U(\mathfrak g)\) acts on \(M(\chi)\) by its Harish-Chandra character. Thus \([M(\chi):L(\psi)]\ne0\) implies \(\psi\in W\cdot\chi\). Dominant integral means \(\chi(h_i)\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}\) for all simple coroots. Central-character equality alone is not sufficient for occurrence.',
 r'The conclusion is explicitly a necessary condition. Renaming the variable in the integrality clause repairs notation without changing content.',
 fate=r'The central-character parametrization and shifted Weyl action are stated in \citet[\S1, pp.~45--47]{brylinski1981differential}.',status='established; weights outside the regular integral case need their own block conventions.',refs=['brylinski1981differential'],pages='P000995-P000996')
add(538,'theorem','Independence of the dominant integral base point',
 r'For \(\chi=w_1(\chi_0+\rho)-\rho\) and \(\psi=w_2(\chi_0+\rho)-\rho\), the multiplicity is asserted to depend only on \(w_1,w_2\), when \(\chi_0\) is dominant integral.',
 r'For \(\lambda,\lambda^{\prime}\) dominant integral and \(x,y\in W\), one has \([M(x\cdot\lambda):L(y\cdot\lambda)]=[M(x\cdot\lambda^{\prime}):L(y\cdot\lambda^{\prime})]\). Here \(\lambda+\rho\) and \(\lambda^{\prime}+\rho\) are strictly dominant, so both infinitesimal characters are regular. Translation equivalences identify these regular integral blocks.',
 r'The unshifted dominant weight may be on a chamber wall: regularity concerns \(\lambda+\rho\). This prevents an erroneous extra requirement that \(\lambda\) itself be strictly dominant.',
 fate=r'This is the translation-principle setting treated in \citet[pp.~42--77]{jantzen1979moduln}; the table of contents and 1979 publication were checked, while the equality also follows from the explicit multiplicity theorem in \citet[\S3]{lusztig1983singularities}.',status='established in regular integral blocks; singular block analogues require parabolic modifications.',refs=['jantzen1979moduln','lusztig1983singularities'],pages='P000996')
add(538,'theorem','Kazhdan--Lusztig values give Verma multiplicities',
 r'The notebook writes \(m(w_1,w_2)=P_{w_1,w_2}(1)\), notes nonnegative polynomial coefficients, and attributes the proof to Beilinson--Bernstein and Brylinski--Kashiwara.',
 r'For a finite Weyl group \(W\), ordinary Kazhdan--Lusztig polynomials normalized by \(P_{x,x}=1\), and a dominant integral \(\lambda\), \([M(x\cdot\lambda):L(y\cdot\lambda)]=P_{x,y}(1)\). The right side is zero unless \(x\le y\) in Bruhat order. This dominant-weight convention matters: formulas written with an antidominant base point or for characters involve changed indices and sometimes the longest element.',
 r'The labels are fixed explicitly; one must not import a longest-element reversal from a differently normalized character formula.',
 fate=r'The notes correctly report a proved theorem. \citet{beilinsonbernstein1981localisation} appeared in 1981 and \citet{brylinskikashiwara1981holonomic} in October 1981. Brylinski\textquotesingle s account derives from the August--September 1980 Toruń meeting. The precise character and multiplicity conventions are compared in \citet[\S3]{lusztig1983singularities}.',status='established well before 23 November 1981; this occurrence is expository rather than a new conjecture.',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',problem='GP-1979-41',refs=['beilinsonbernstein1981localisation','brylinskikashiwara1981holonomic','brylinski1981differential','lusztig1983singularities'],pages='P000996')
add(538,'question','What the coefficients remember beyond total multiplicity',
 r'After \(P(q)=a_0+a_1q+\cdots\), the notes seek an interpretation of the individual \(a_i\). This question precedes the Beilinson heading.',
 r'The coefficient question asks for a natural filtration or geometric grading whose graded multiplicities recover \(P_{x,y}(q)\), rather than only its value at \(q=1\). In the representation-theoretic answer, Jantzen layers occur with a length-dependent shift and twice the polynomial degree; see 1981-540.6. Intersection cohomology gives a separate grading; see 1981-540.7.',
 r'This is a question about a refinement of the already proved multiplicity theorem, not a second statement of the same conjecture. The notebook\textquotesingle s subsequent formula does not correctly normalize that refinement.',
 cls='B',fate=r'The filtration refinement was established by \citet{beilinsonbernstein1993jantzen}. The 1981 intermediate state is documented in \citet[Introduction]{gabberjoseph1981towards}; no verified prepublication date for the full 1993 theorem was found.',status='settled for ordinary Verma modules with the usual dominant deformation; it does not assert arbitrary-direction invariance.',problem='GP-1979-41',refs=['beilinsonbernstein1993jantzen','gabberjoseph1981towards'],pages='P000996')
save()
add(539,'definition','Category O and the sign of the nilradical',
 r'Beilinson defines a category of finitely generated modules with finite-dimensional Cartan behavior and local nilpotence of a nilradical. A marginal correction says \ru{Здесь только \(\mathfrak n_+\), а не \(\mathfrak n_-\), но это безразлично}.',
 r'For the chosen Borel, \(\mathcal O\) consists of finitely generated \(U(\mathfrak g)\)-modules that are \(\mathfrak h\)-semisimple and locally \(U(\mathfrak n_+)\)-finite. Their weight spaces are finite dimensional; positive root vectors act locally nilpotently. Reversing the Borel gives an equivalent category, but does not make local nilpotence of \(\mathfrak n_+\) and \(\mathfrak n_-\) equivalent on the same module.',
 r'The marginal phrase is defensible only as a choice-of-Borel convention. On a generic Verma module, the lowering generators are not locally nilpotent.',
 fate=r'The original definition and basic properties occur in \citet[\S1]{bernsteingelfandgelfand1976category}, received 22 December 1975 and published in 1976. Its full Russian text was inspected.',status='a standard abelian highest-weight category, with the chosen positive nilradical part of its definition.',refs=['bernsteingelfandgelfand1976category'],pages='P000996')
add(539,'construction','Restricted contragredient duality',
 r'The notes discuss the dual of a Verma module, warn that the full dual can be too large, and select functions transforming finite-dimensionally under \(\mathfrak h\).',
 r'If \(M=\bigoplus_\mu M_\mu\in\mathcal O\), set \(dM=\bigoplus_\mu M_\mu^*\), not the product \(\prod_\mu M_\mu^*\). With the Chevalley anti-involution \(\tau(e_i)=f_i\), \(\tau(f_i)=e_i\), \(\tau(h)=h\), define \((x\phi)(m)=\phi(\tau(x)m)\). Then \(d\) is an exact contravariant involution of \(\mathcal O\), and \(dM(\chi)\) is the costandard module. An ordinary untwisted dual has different highest-weight conventions.',
 r'The restriction to the direct sum of weight-space duals and the anti-involution are essential additions. The notebook does not define the action on its functional subspace.',
 fate=r'This is the duality of \citet[\S1]{bernsteingelfandgelfand1976category}; it is precisely what permits the contravariant-form construction in the next intervention.',status='established; the full algebraic dual is generally outside category O.',refs=['bernsteingelfandgelfand1976category'],pages='P000996')
save()
add(540,'construction','The normalized contravariant Shapovalov form',
 r'A form is defined on monomials \(f_{i_1}\cdots f_{i_k}v\), with zero for different “unordered sets” of indices; the next page calls it \ru{инвар. отн. алг. Ли}.',
 r'The normalized form on \(M(\chi)\) satisfies \(S_\chi(v_\chi,v_\chi)=1\) and \(S_\chi(xm,n)=S_\chi(m,\tau(x)n)\), where \(\tau\) is the Chevalley anti-involution. Thus \[S_\chi(uv_\chi,wv_\chi)=\chi(\operatorname{HC}(\tau(u)w)).\] Here the Harish-Chandra projection is followed by evaluation on \(U(\mathfrak h)\). Distinct weight spaces are orthogonal. Since \(\tau\) reverses products, \(\tau(f_{i_1}\cdots f_{i_k})=e_{i_k}\cdots e_{i_1}\).',
 r'“Invariant” must mean contravariant, not \(S(xm,n)+S(m,xn)=0\). The orthogonality test is equality of total root weight, hence of multiplicities of simple generators, not equality of sets with repetitions discarded.',
 fate=r'The named form predates the talk: \citet{shapovalov1972bilinear}, received 15 March 1972. Its deformation use is explicit in \citet[\S1]{gabberjoseph1981towards}. The Shapovalov publisher record was checked; the Gabber--Joseph construction was read.',status='standard; the notebook omits the anti-involution and uses imprecise orthogonality and invariance terminology.',correct='false as written',refs=['shapovalov1972bilinear','gabberjoseph1981towards'],pages='P000996-P000997')
add(540,'construction','PBW trivialization of the family of Verma modules',
 r'The notebook identifies \(f_{i_1}\cdots f_{i_l}v_\chi\) with the same monomial applied to \(v_{\chi^{\prime}}\).',
 r'PBW gives a vector-space isomorphism \(U(\mathfrak n_-)\simeq M(\chi)\), \(u\mapsto uv_\chi\), for every \(\chi\). Consequently all Verma modules can be put on one underlying vector space, while the action of \(\mathfrak h\oplus\mathfrak n_+\), and the contravariant form, vary with the parameter. This is not an isomorphism of \(\mathfrak g\)-modules when highest weights differ.',
 r'The simple-root monomials span but are not a PBW basis without an ordering and composite-root vectors. PBW proves the proposed correspondence is well defined despite that presentation.',
 fate=r'The deformation framework uses precisely this universal induced-module construction; see \citet[\S1]{gabberjoseph1981towards}.',status='valid as a vector-space trivialization.',refs=['gabberjoseph1981towards'],pages='P000997')
add(540,'claim','Generic nondegeneracy along the rho deformation',
 r'For \(M(\chi+t\rho)\), the notes assert that the forms \((\ ,\ )_t\) are nondegenerate for small \(t\ne0\).',
 r'For a finite-dimensional semisimple \(\mathfrak g\), reducibility of \(M(\chi+t\rho)\) can occur only when \(\langle\chi+\rho+t\rho,\alpha^\vee\rangle\in\mathbb Z_{>0}\) for a positive root \(\alpha\). Since \(\langle\rho,\alpha^\vee\rangle>0\), the exceptional values form a locally discrete subset of \(\mathbb C\). Thus a sufficiently small punctured disk about zero contains none. Equivalently, the form is nondegenerate over \(\mathbb C(t)\).',
 r'The formulation supplies the parameter field and the finite-root-system hypothesis. For a general direction \(\gamma\), it must at least be transverse to any reducibility hyperplane containing \(\chi\).',
 fate=r'This is the determinant/deformation method underlying \citet{shapovalov1972bilinear} and \citet[\S1]{gabberjoseph1981towards}; it is not the later theorem about filtration multiplicities.',status='correct in the finite-dimensional setting with the stated regular deformation.',refs=['shapovalov1972bilinear','gabberjoseph1981towards'],pages='P000997')
add(540,'definition','The Jantzen filtration requires parameter-dependent lifts',
 r'The literal definition is \(M_i=\{x\in M_\chi:(x,y)_t=O(t^i)\text{ for all }y\in M(\chi)\}\), with vectors regarded as fixed under the PBW identification. The next sentence says these are \(\mathfrak g\)-submodules.',
 r'Put \(A=\mathbb C[t]_{(t)}\) and let \(M_A\) be the deformed Verma module of highest weight \(\chi+t\gamma\). Define \(M_A^{(i)}=\{m\in M_A:S_A(m,M_A)\subset t^iA\}\) and \(J^iM=(M_A^{(i)}+tM_A)/tM_A\). Equivalently, a vector of \(M\) belongs to \(J^i\) if it admits a lift depending on \(t\) whose pairing is divisible by \(t^i\). Contravariance makes these \(\mathfrak g\)-submodules; \(J^1\) is the radical of the specialized form.',
 r'Moving lifts are indispensable. A constant lift need not realize the maximal order of vanishing, so the notebook\textquotesingle s displayed set is not a complete definition of the Jantzen filtration.',
 fate=r'This is Jantzen\textquotesingle s pre-1981 construction, used in \citet[\S1]{gabberjoseph1981towards}; see also \citet[pp.~112--181]{jantzen1979moduln} for its original book placement.',status='established with the lattice-and-specialization definition; a fixed-vector reading can give the wrong filtration.',correct='false as written',refs=['gabberjoseph1981towards','jantzen1979moduln'],pages='P000997')
add(540,'question','Does changing the deformation direction preserve layer multiplicities?',
 r'The exact question is \ru{Что если сменить \(\rho\)? Видимо, кратн. не будут меняться.} No cone or genericity condition on the replacement is written.',
 r'For a fixed Verma module, compare \([J^i_\gamma M/J^{i+1}_\gamma M:L(\psi)]\) as the regular direction \(\gamma\) varies. The dominant-direction theorem does not extend to every regular direction. In type \(A_3\), take highest weight \(-\alpha_1-\alpha_3\) and examine the weight lower by \(2\alpha_1+3\alpha_2+2\alpha_3\). Its dimension is 13. The layer dimensions for \(\gamma=\rho\) are \((7,3,2,1)\); for a regular \(\gamma\) satisfying \(\gamma(h_1+2h_2+h_3)=0\), they are \((7,2,4,0)\). For example, Dynkin coordinates \((1,-2,3)\) satisfy that relation and are nonzero on every positive coroot.',
 r'The unrestricted question is reconstructed literally, while the positive answer under a dominant-cone restriction is separated from it. The explicit counterexample is from the later literature, not an unrecorded qualification attributed to S. Gelfand.',
 cls='B',fate=r'\citet[\S\S1.4--1.6 and 8.4]{williamson2016local} proves dominant local Hodge theory and gives this counterexample. The preprint first circulated on 8 October 2014; the journal volume is dated 2016, with online publication on 7 July 2017. \citet{beilinsonbernstein1993jantzen} had proved the dominant-direction Jantzen conjectures.',status='the unrestricted expectation is false; the dominant-direction version is established. This is a settled problem, not a C/D opportunity.',correct='false as written',problem='GP-1981-10',refs=['williamson2016local','beilinsonbernstein1993jantzen'],pages='P000997')
add(540,'claim','Correct grading of Kazhdan--Lusztig coefficients in Jantzen layers',
 r'The notes identify \(P_{w_1,w_2}(q)\) with \(\sum_iq^i[L_\psi:M_i/M_{i+1}]\). The scan confirms the reversed order of the multiplicity brackets.',
 r'With \(\lambda\) dominant integral, \(x,y\in W\), the usual Jantzen filtration from a dominant regular deformation satisfies \[\sum_{j\ge0}[J^jM(x\cdot\lambda)/J^{j+1}M(x\cdot\lambda):L(y\cdot\lambda)]v^j=v^{\ell(y)-\ell(x)}P_{x,y}(v^{-2}).\] Thus a coefficient of \(q^i\) belongs to layer \(\ell(y)-\ell(x)-2i\). For \(\mathfrak{sl}_2\), \(P_{e,s}=1\), whereas the lower simple constituent lies in layer 1, already disproving the literal unshifted formula.',
 r'Two separate repairs are required: put the module before the simple object in the multiplicity bracket, and include the length shift and doubled polynomial degree. The rank-one diagnostic follows immediately from the single singular vector.',
 cls='B',fate=r'The graded theorem is part of \citet{beilinsonbernstein1993jantzen}; \citet[\S1.4]{williamson2016local} states the equality with the grading filtration under dominant deformation. In November 1981 this refinement remained beyond the already proved ungraded multiplicity theorem.',status='established with these conventions. Summing layers gives 1981-538.8, but does not justify the notebook\textquotesingle s degree assignment.',correct='false as written',problem='GP-1979-41',refs=['beilinsonbernstein1993jantzen','williamson2016local'],pages='P000997')
add(540,'claim','Local intersection cohomology of Schubert closures',
 r'The coefficients are described as \(a_i=\dim H^i\) of one Schubert cell near another, with \ru{локальн. когомол. норм. сечения}; a later line writes ``cell contained in other cell\textquotesingle.',
 r'Let \(X_y=\overline{ByB/B}\subset G/B\), \(x\le y\), and \(p_x\in BxB/B\). Normalize the intersection complex \(IC^0(X_y)\) so that it restricts to the constant sheaf in degree zero on the smooth locus. Then \(P_{x,y}(q)=\sum_{i\ge0}\dim\mathcal H^{2i}(IC^0(X_y))_{p_x}q^i\); odd stalk groups vanish. A transverse normal slice gives the same local singularity data, with the appropriate intersection-complex normalization.',
 r'Distinct Schubert cells are disjoint. The intended containment is a cell inside another cell\textquotesingle s closure, and the groups are intersection cohomology stalks, not arbitrary ordinary local cohomology. The degree is \(2i\), not \(i\).',
 fate=r'This interpretation was already in \citet{kazhdanlusztig1980schubert}; the statement and normalization were checked in \citet[Theorem~4.1]{lusztig1983singularities}. It belongs with the earlier occurrence 1979-484.4.',status=r'established geometric interpretation; the missing word ``intersection\textquotesingle\ is mathematically decisive.',correct='false as written',problem='GP-1979-41',refs=['kazhdanlusztig1980schubert','lusztig1983singularities'],pages='P000997',area='Geometric representation theory')
save()
add(541,'definition','Weights and multiplicities in a finite-dimensional simple module',
 r'The source writes \(L(\chi)=\bigoplus_\lambda L(\chi)_\lambda\) but labels the summand as the weight space of weight \(\chi\).',
 r'For dominant integral highest weight \(\chi\), the finite-dimensional module \(L(\chi)\) decomposes into \(L(\chi)_\lambda=\{v:hv=\lambda(h)v\ \forall h\in\mathfrak h\}\). Its weight multiplicity is \(m(\chi,\lambda)=\dim L(\chi)_\lambda\). The highest weight labels the representation, whereas \(\lambda\) labels a summand.',
 r'The annotation must say weight \(\lambda\), as the subscript and the formula on the next page independently show.',
 fate=r'This is the finite-dimensional setup used in \citet[\S6]{lusztig1983singularities}, not the Verma composition multiplicity of record 538.',status='standard, with one faithful variable slip in the manuscript.',correct='false as written',refs=['lusztig1983singularities'],pages='P000997-P000998')
add(541,'theorem','Type-A alternants and the missing highest-weight shift',
 r'Gelfand gives the ratio of determinants with numerator exponents \(a_i\), denominator exponents \(0,1,\ldots,n-1\), then identifies \(\chi=(a_1,\ldots,a_n)\).',
 r'For \(GL_n\), dominant integral \(\lambda_1\ge\cdots\ge\lambda_n\) gives \[\operatorname{ch}L(\lambda)=\frac{\det(x_j^{\lambda_i+n-i})_{i,j=1}^n}{\det(x_j^{n-i})_{i,j=1}^n}.\] Reversing rows in numerator and denominator yields the ascending-denominator convention of the manuscript, with \(a_i=\lambda_{n+1-i}+i-1\). The determinant quotient is a symmetric Laurent polynomial; setting \(\lambda=0\) gives 1.',
 r'The \(\rho\)-shift cannot be omitted. If the \(a_i\) literally equal the unshifted coordinates of the highest weight, even the trivial representation gives a zero numerator when \(n>1\).',
 fate=r'This is the classical alternant form of the Weyl character formula; its general form is recalled in \citet[\S6, (6.3)]{lusztig1983singularities}, and the determinant/Schur-function identification in \citet[\S2.1]{krattenthaler1999determinant}.',status='established; the displayed ratio requires shifted exponents and a stated row-order convention.',correct='false as written',refs=['lusztig1983singularities','krattenthaler1999determinant'],pages='P000997-P000998',speaker='I. M. Gelfand')
add(541,'problem','Cauchy determinant exercise',
 r'Gelfand asks first-year students to calculate \(\det(1/(\lambda_i-\mu_j))\), under the sole restriction \(\lambda_i\ne\mu_j\).',
 r'For all cross-distinct parameters, \[\det\left(\frac1{\lambda_i-\mu_j}\right)_{i,j=1}^n=\frac{\prod_{i<j}(\lambda_j-\lambda_i)(\mu_i-\mu_j)}{\prod_{i,j}(\lambda_i-\mu_j)}.\] Coincidences within the \(\lambda\)-list or within the \(\mu\)-list are permitted and make both sides zero. Multiplying by the denominator gives an alternating polynomial in each list; divisibility by the two Vandermonde factors and comparison of one coefficient determine the formula.',
 r'The solution and its sign are supplied as an annotation to an explicitly elementary exercise; no research problem is manufactured from it.',
 fate=r'This classical double alternant appears in \citet[\S2.1, (2.7)]{krattenthaler1999determinant}, with plus-denominator parameters. Substituting \(Y_j=-\mu_j\) gives exactly the source convention.',status='long settled; the seminar wording is a pedagogical problem.',correct='correct',refs=['krattenthaler1999determinant'],pages='P000998',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',area='Algebraic combinatorics')
add(541,'theorem','Finite weight multiplicities from affine Kazhdan--Lusztig polynomials',
 r'Zelevinsky writes \(m(\lambda,\chi)=P_{n_\lambda,n_\chi}(1)\) and labels the indices as affine Weyl group elements, without defining them. This swaps the argument order used immediately above.',
 r'Let \(\mathfrak g\) be complex simple, \(P\) its weight lattice, and \(\lambda,\mu\) dominant integral with \(\mu\le\lambda\). Use the extended affine Weyl group \(W\ltimes P\) in Lusztig\textquotesingle s convention, and let \(n_\eta\) be the longest element of \(Wt_\eta W\). Then \(\dim L(\lambda)_\mu=P_{n_\mu,n_\lambda}(1)\). For an arbitrary integral weight, first replace it by its dominant Weyl-orbit representative. The root datum and translation lattice matter; ``affine Weyl group\textquotesingle\ alone is insufficient for arbitrary \(P\).',
 r'The definition of \(n_\eta\), dominance conditions, and consistent highest-weight/weight argument order come from the identified publication, not from the notebook. Ordinary finite-Weyl KL polynomials from record 538 are not sufficient here.',
 cls='B',fate=r'An exact match is \citet[\S2 and Theorem~6.1]{lusztig1983singularities}. The volume records a Luminy conference of 6--10 July 1981, but that fact alone does not verify when this particular theorem first circulated. The November notebook demonstrates prepublication knowledge; the exact printed result is dated 1983. Class B records the later publication by another mathematician, not priority for the seminar.',status='established with the specified extended affine indices; it is a different multiplicity problem from the Verma theorem.',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=['lusztig1983singularities'],pages='P000998',area='Geometric representation theory')
add(541,'conjecture','An unspecified extension to all Kac--Moody algebras',
 r'The next line says \ru{То же \(\forall\) алгебр Кац--Муди}; the entire subsequent expression \(\operatorname{ch}L_\lambda(K_\mu)=P_{\lambda,\mu}(q)\) is crossed out.',
 r'The surviving text supports only a proposed Kac--Moody generalization. It does not specify a symmetrizable root datum, the relevant category, the role of \(q\), the elements \(n_\lambda\), or whether it concerns weight multiplicities or highest-weight composition multiplicities. There is therefore no single well-defined proposition to test.',
 r'It would be unjustified to replace the erased expression by a modern Kac--Moody character theorem. In infinite type there need not be a longest element of a finite-Weyl double coset of the kind used in 1981-541.4.',
 cls='F',fate=r'\citet[\S5 and \S6]{lusztig1983singularities} distinguishes several different extensions involving affine polynomials. The search found no exact identification of this erased formula. General Kac--Moody character results provide context only.',status='not identifiable at the level required for a problem-index entry; no conclusion of openness or nondevelopment is warranted.',correct='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',refs=['lusztig1983singularities'],confidence='low',pages='P000998',area='Kac--Moody representation theory')
save()
add(542,'definition','Primitivity of the universal enveloping algebra',
 r'For a linear algebraic group \(G\subset GL_n(\mathbb C)\), the notes define \(U(L(G))\) to be primitive when zero is a primitive ideal, equivalently the kernel of a faithful irreducible representation. ``Irreducible\textquotesingle\ is inserted above the line.',
 r'An associative unital algebra \(A\) is left primitive if it has a faithful simple left module; equivalently, \(0=\operatorname{Ann}_A(M)\) for a simple module \(M\). Applied to \(A=U(\mathfrak g)\), this requires faithfulness of the enveloping-algebra action, a stronger condition than faithfulness merely on the Lie subspace \(\mathfrak g\). The representation need not be finite dimensional.',
 r'The insertion in the manuscript is mathematically essential. Every algebra has a faithful regular module, which would make the definition vacuous without simplicity.',
 fate=r'This is the definition used in \citet[\S0]{elashvili1982frobenius}. That announcement was received on 30 November 1981, one week after the seminar, and published in 1982.',status='a standard ring-theoretic definition.',correct='correct',refs=['elashvili1982frobenius'],pages='P000998',area='Lie algebras and primitive ideals')
add(542,'question',"Jacobson's primitive-envelope question for algebraic Lie algebras",
 r'The notes ask which \(G\) and \(L(G)\) have primitive enveloping algebra, attribute the question to Jacobson, and add ``last year\textquotesingle\ below his name.',
 r'For a finite-dimensional algebraic Lie algebra \(\mathfrak g=\operatorname{Lie}(G)\) over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, the following are equivalent: \(U(\mathfrak g)\) is primitive; the coadjoint action has a Zariski-open orbit; and some \(f\in\mathfrak g^*\) makes \(B_f(x,y)=f([x,y])\) nondegenerate. A Lie algebra satisfying the last condition is called Frobenius. One may replace \(G\) by its identity component without changing the criterion.',
 r'The algebraicity hypothesis is retained: an open-orbit criterion for this problem must not silently be asserted for arbitrary nonalgebraic Lie algebras. The marginal date is not reliable evidence that Jacobson first posed the question in 1980.',
 fate=r'\citet[\S0, theorem attributed to Ooms]{elashvili1982frobenius} explicitly dates the answer to 1976 and cites Ooms\textquotesingle s supplements in Proc. Amer. Math. Soc. 58, 67--72. The 1976 full text was not accessible in this run, so the theorem was verified through Elashvili\textquotesingle s exact statement. The question already occurs as 1964-37.1 in the supplied index.',status='settled before this seminar for algebraic Lie algebras; the bibliographic attribution is stronger than a topical match.',problem='GP-1981-12',refs=['elashvili1982frobenius'],pages='P000998',area='Lie algebras and primitive ideals')
add(542,'claim','Why nonzero semisimple and nilpotent Lie algebras are excluded',
 r'The source says semisimple algebras, and an inserted parenthetical ``nilpotent ones\textquotesingle, are unsuitable.',
 r'A nonzero complex semisimple Lie algebra is not Frobenius: every coadjoint stabilizer has dimension at least its positive rank. A nonzero nilpotent Lie algebra has nonzero center, which lies in the radical of every \(B_f\). Hence neither class has primitive enveloping algebra under the algebraic criterion. The zero Lie algebra is an exception: \(U(0)=\mathbb C\) is primitive.',
 r'The qualification ``nonzero\textquotesingle\ repairs the trivial edge case. The two exclusions have different mechanisms; nilpotence by itself is not being confused with associative nilpotence of \(U(\mathfrak g)\).',
 fate=r'These deductions are immediate applications of the Ooms criterion printed in \citet[\S0]{elashvili1982frobenius}; no new classification is asserted.',status='established with the zero-algebra exception.',refs=['elashvili1982frobenius'],pages='P000998',area='Lie algebras and primitive ideals')
add(542,'construction','The affine Lie algebra is Frobenius',
 r'The first example is the Lie algebra of the affine group, represented by matrices whose last column is zero.',
 r'For \(n\ge1\), \(\mathfrak{aff}_n=\mathfrak{gl}_n\ltimes\mathbb C^n\), with the standard action and abelian translation ideal, is Frobenius and therefore has primitive enveloping algebra. The usual column-vector convention realizes it by matrices \(\left(\begin{smallmatrix}A&b\\0&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)\). The notebook\textquotesingle s last-column-zero matrices give an isomorphic Lie algebra via minus transpose, so they are not a mathematical error.',
 r'The row/column convention is stated explicitly. For the Frobenius assertion, the open orbit of \(GL_n\) on nonzero covectors has stabilizer algebra isomorphic to \(\mathfrak{aff}_{n-1}\); iteration reduces to the zero algebra.',
 fate=r'This is the first reduced family in \citet[\S2, table]{elashvili1982frobenius}; the stabilizer criterion is its \S1 lemma. The two-dimensional base case was already noted in 1964-37.2.',status='established for all positive n; the source\textquotesingle s matrix convention is retained as valid.',refs=['elashvili1982frobenius'],pages='P000998',area='Lie algebras and primitive ideals')
add(542,'construction','The binary-cubic semidirect product',
 r'The second example is \(\mathfrak{gl}_2\ltimes V_4\), with abelian \(V_4\), called the unique four-dimensional irreducible representation.',
 r'Take \(V_4=\operatorname{Sym}^3\mathbb C^2\), with the natural \(\mathfrak{gl}_2\)-action. Then \(\mathfrak{gl}_2\ltimes V_4\) is Frobenius. A generic binary cubic has three distinct projective roots; its stabilizer in \(GL_2\) is finite, so the infinitesimal orbit has dimension four and is open in \(V_4\) (or its dual). The abelian semidirect-product criterion then applies with zero generic stabilizer.',
 r'The irreducible four-dimensional \(\mathfrak{sl}_2\)-module is unique, but extensions to \(\mathfrak{gl}_2\) depend on the central character. A zero central action leaves a central ideal and cannot be Frobenius. Nonzero scalar central actions yield isomorphic Lie semidirect products after rescaling the central generator; the natural cubic action is one choice.',
 fate=r'This is the second reduced family in \citet[\S2, table]{elashvili1982frobenius}. The publication provides an exact example match, while the notebook\textquotesingle s uniqueness language needs the stated qualification.',status='established with nontrivial action of the scalar center.',correct='false as written',refs=['elashvili1982frobenius'],pages='P000998',area='Lie algebras and primitive ideals')
add(542,'theorem','The asserted two-example classification and its missing reductive hypotheses',
 r'The notebook claims there are no other examples for \(L=R\ltimes V\), with \(R\) simple, \(V\) abelian and irreducible. The word preceding ``simple\textquotesingle\ is crossed out.',
 r'The literal hypothesis cannot include either displayed \(\mathfrak{gl}_n\) example, since \(\mathfrak{gl}_n\) is not simple. The identified paper classifies reduced algebraic Frobenius triples \((R,V,\varphi)\) with \(R\) reductive and with either irreducible \(V\), or abelian \(V\) and simple derived algebra \([R,R]\). Its table has ten families. Under the simultaneous narrow requirements of irreducible \(V\) and simple derived algebra, the first two reduced families are the affine standard module and the binary cubic; the \(n=1\) affine edge case has zero derived algebra.',
 r'This is a comparison with the exact later announcement, not a claim that the erased word can be read as ``reductive\textquotesingle. Reduction and the distinction between \(R\) and \([R,R]\) are substantive missing hypotheses.',
 cls='A',fate=r'\citet[\S0 and \S2]{elashvili1982frobenius}, received 30 November 1981 and printed in 1982, is a close chronological and mathematical match. Its broader ten-family theorem prevents the seminar\textquotesingle s compressed ``no others\textquotesingle\ from being quoted without qualification.',status='the relevant reduced classification is published; the literal simple-R formulation is false/incompatible with its examples.',correct='false as written',match='partial',refs=['elashvili1982frobenius'],pages='P000998',area='Lie algebras and primitive ideals')
add(542,'method','Castling as a method for generating further Frobenius examples',
 r'The intervention ends: there is a method for constructing examples beyond these. No method is named or written out.',
 r'A plausible precise identification is the castling operation in the ensuing publication. If \(\dim V=m\) and \(0<n<m\), compare \((R\oplus\mathfrak{sl}_n,V\otimes\mathbb C^n,\varphi\otimes\mathrm{std})\) and \((R\oplus\mathfrak{sl}_{m-n},V^*\otimes\mathbb C^{m-n},\varphi^*\otimes\mathrm{std})\). The associated abelian semidirect products are simultaneously Frobenius. A reduced triple minimizes the module dimension in its castling class.',
 r'The notebook supplies only the existence of a method. Identification with castling is supported by the speaker\textquotesingle s paper, but remains a reconstruction; the displayed transformation is not transcribed from the seminar.',
 cls='A',fate=r'\citet[\S2, lemma and definition of \ru{приведённая} triples]{elashvili1982frobenius} gives exactly this operation and uses it for the classification. The paper explicitly traces it to Sato--Kimura transformations.',status='a published construction; the connection to the unnamed seminar remark is probable, not demonstrable from that remark alone.',match='partial',refs=['elashvili1982frobenius'],confidence='medium',pages='P000998',area='Lie algebras and primitive ideals')
add(542,'remark','An unidentified gamma function associated with the cubic example',
 r'Next to \(\mathfrak{gl}_2\ltimes V_4\), a marginal note reads that another interesting \(\Gamma\)-function arises here.',
 r'No function, integral, functional equation, normalization, or parameter is recorded. Possible connections to relative invariants and local zeta integrals cannot determine a unique gamma function from this line.',
 r'The symbol is visibly \(\Gamma\), but neither the manuscript nor the short Frobenius announcement supplies enough information to reconstruct the object.',
 cls='F',fate=r'The full \citet{elashvili1982frobenius} was inspected; it classifies Frobenius algebras and mentions Sato--Kimura, but contains no matching gamma-function formula. Searches combining Elashvili, Frobenius, binary cubics and gamma functions produced no exact identification.',status='unidentified, not an established open problem.',correct='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',refs=['elashvili1982frobenius'],confidence='low',pages='P000998',area='Special functions')
save()
add(543,'problem','The singular limit of the van der Pol equation',
 r'Gelfand writes \(\varepsilon\ddot x+(x^2-1)\dot x+x=0\) and asks what happens when \(\varepsilon\to0\). The heading names Reeb and, underneath, Cartier\textquotesingle s November 1981 Bourbaki survey.',
 r'Take \(\varepsilon>0\) and let it decrease to zero. The second-order equation loses one derivative when \(\varepsilon=0\), so naive substitution cannot accommodate two arbitrary initial data or the rapid transitions. A precise version asks for the limiting attracting periodic orbit and the matching of its slow segments across fast jumps; 1981-543.3 states this version. A claim of uniform convergence for all initial data on all times is not supported.',
 r'The notes ask a broad singular-limit question. The periodic-orbit formulation is selected because the sketches show relaxation jumps and because it is the actual question treated in the identified source.',
 fate=r'\citet[\S1 and \S5, nos.~21--23]{cartier1982perturbations} is an exact match, delivered in November 1981 and printed in 1982. Cartier reports methods of Reeb\textquotesingle s school; Reeb was not the speaker of Bourbaki exposé 580.',status='the classical relaxation limit in this formulation is established; the seminar reports a contemporary exposition of existing and recent methods.',problem='GP-1981-13',refs=['cartier1982perturbations'],pages='P000999',area='Singular perturbation and nonstandard analysis')
add(543,'construction','Integrating the reduced first-order equation',
 r'At \(\varepsilon=0\), the notes write \(dx/dt=-x/(x^2-1)\), separate variables, and obtain \(t+C=\log|x|-x^2/2\).',
 r'On an interval where \(x\ne0,\pm1\), the reduced equation is \(\dot x=x/(1-x^2)\), hence \(d(\log|x|-x^2/2)/dt=1\). The equilibrium \(x\equiv0\) must be restored after division by \(x\). A finite derivative cannot satisfy the reduced equation at \(x=\pm1\); those turning points require a fast transition in the full equation.',
 r'The division conditions and omitted equilibrium are supplied. The implicit formula parametrizes individual slow branches, not a complete periodic solution.',
 fate=r'The same reduction is given in \citet[\S5, no.~21, equation~(17)]{cartier1982perturbations}. The derivative check is elementary and confirms the signs in both transcriptions.',status='correct with the excluded points and equilibrium explicitly recorded.',refs=['cartier1982perturbations'],pages='P000999',area='Singular perturbation and nonstandard analysis')
add(543,'claim','Relaxation jumps and the limiting periodic cycle',
 r'Two sketches have nearly vertical segments, accompanied by the statement that limiting solutions have such jumps.',
 r'Write \(F(x)=x^3/3-x\) and \(y=\varepsilon\dot x+F(x)\), giving \(\varepsilon\dot x=y-F(x)\), \(\dot y=-x\). As \(\varepsilon\downarrow0\), the attracting periodic orbit approaches the slow arcs \(y=F(x)\), \(x\in[1,2]\) and \(x\in[-2,-1]\), connected by fast segments at \(y=-2/3\) and \(y=2/3\). In \(x(t)\), the jumps are \(1\to-2\) and \(-1\to2\). Convergence of the phase-plane cycle is understood geometrically; time-parametrized convergence is local away from jumps after fixing phase.',
 r'The sketch does not label the endpoints or specify the topology of convergence. These details are reconstructed from the polynomial slow manifold and the matched-cycle analysis, not from the drawing alone.',
 fate=r'\citet[\S5, nos.~22--23 and Figure~5]{cartier1982perturbations} gives this Liénard-plane construction and the attracting limit cycle. The November 1981 exposition precedes the Moscow report; the 1982 print date does not make it a new Moscow conjecture.',status='established relaxation asymptotics; uniform convergence of continuous trajectories to a discontinuous graph across a jump is not the asserted theorem.',refs=['cartier1982perturbations'],pages='P000999',area='Singular perturbation and nonstandard analysis',problem='GP-1981-13')
add(543,'construction','Rayleigh and van der Pol equations are linked by differentiation',
 r'The second displayed equation is \(\varepsilon\ddot u+(\dot u^3/3-\dot u)+u=0\), called Rayleigh\textquotesingle s equation, and is said to reduce to van der Pol.',
 r'If \(u\) solves Rayleigh\textquotesingle s equation and \(x=\dot u\), differentiation gives \(\varepsilon\ddot x+(x^2-1)\dot x+x=0\). Conversely, from a van der Pol solution define \(u=-\varepsilon\dot x-x^3/3+x\). Then the van der Pol equation gives \(\dot u=x\), and substitution verifies Rayleigh\textquotesingle s equation. For smooth solutions with \(\varepsilon\ne0\), these formulas give the precise correspondence without an arbitrary integration constant.',
 r'The substitution and converse are supplied to make ``reduces\textquotesingle\ exact. Cartier subsequently changes a sign in his Liénard coordinate convention; that does not change this displayed differentiation identity.',
 fate=r'The two equations and the derivative relation are written in \citet[\S5, no.~21]{cartier1982perturbations}.',status='an established elementary equivalence, with the variable transformation explicit.',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=['cartier1982perturbations'],pages='P000999',area='Singular perturbation and nonstandard analysis')
add(543,'method','Using an infinitesimal parameter in singular perturbation theory',
 r'The notebook emphasizes that equation (1) is studied by nonstandard analysis.',
 r'In a suitable nonstandard enlargement of the real field and its function spaces, choose positive infinitesimal \(\varepsilon\), analyze the internal differential equation, and take standard parts on limited slow portions. Different rescalings resolve fast portions. Transfer and the precise internal/external distinction are required; the mere existence of a field containing infinitesimals does not prove a singular-limit theorem.',
 r'This is a method, not a claim that setting an ordinary parameter equal to zero is legitimate. The later surreal-number sketch is a different construction and does not itself provide the enlargement or transfer principle.',
 fate=r'\citet[\S1, no.~2; \S3; \S5]{cartier1982perturbations} explains the infinitesimal method and its application to relaxation oscillations. The report is specifically about Reeb\textquotesingle s school, as presented by Cartier in November 1981.',status='an established framework for these asymptotics; its foundations must not be conflated with arbitrary ordered-field extensions.',refs=['cartier1982perturbations'],pages='P000999',area='Singular perturbation and nonstandard analysis')
add(543,'construction','The zero-one probability space and ultrapower quotient',
 r'The scan and both transcriptions give \(p:2^\Omega\to(0,1)\), finite additivity, and equivalence of \(x^{\prime},x^{\prime\prime}:\Omega\to X\) when \(P(x^{\prime}=x^{\prime\prime})=1\).',
 r'The intended construction uses a normalized finitely additive measure \(p:2^\Omega\to\{0,1\}\), with \(p(\Omega)=1\). Then \(\mathcal U=\{A:p(A)=1\}\) is an ultrafilter, and \(X^\Omega/\mathcal U\) identifies maps equal on a \(\mathcal U\)-large set. For \(X=\mathbb R\), operations descend pointwise. Taking \(\Omega=\mathbb N\) and a nonprincipal ultrafilter produces a nontrivial ultrapower in which \([1/n]\) is positive infinitesimal.',
 r'The codomain is inferred from Cartier\textquotesingle s exact construction, not visually read as braces: the notebook truly has round parentheses. Finite additivity forces \(p(\varnothing)=0\), and the equivalence relation requires the value 1, so the open interval is impossible. Replacing it merely by \([0,1]\) would allow an ordinary probability algebra whose quotient need not be a field; the zero-one condition is decisive.',
 fate=r'\citet[\S2, no.~3, assumptions (a)--(c), and no.~4]{cartier1982perturbations} explicitly requires values zero or one and quotienting random elements by almost-sure equality. This exact source resolves the manuscript error.',status='the ultrapower construction is established after this emendation; normalization and a nonprincipal choice are additionally needed for the intended infinitesimals.',correct='false as written',refs=['cartier1982perturbations'],pages='P000999',area='Logic and nonstandard analysis')
add(543,'construction','Surreal numbers represented by transfinite sign sequences',
 r'Under the heading \ru{Числа Крускала}, the notes call the construction a simple field extending \(\mathbb R\), identify the empty word with 0, up with 1, down with \(-1\), and give \(n\) ups, up-down \(=1/2\), and up-down-up \(=3/4\).',
 r'These are surreal sign expansions: a surreal number is a function from an ordinal to \(\{+,-\}\), ordered lexicographically with \(-<\text{undefined}<+\) at the first differing position. The empty sequence is zero. Constant finite sequences give integers, and finite mixed sequences give dyadic rationals. With Conway\textquotesingle s recursively defined operations, the full class \(\mathbf{No}\) is a real-closed ordered field in the proper-class sense, containing copies of the reals and ordinals.',
 r'The identification with surreal numbers is strong; ``Kruskal\textquotesingle\ records the seminar\textquotesingle s attribution, not the standard origin of the field. The proper-class qualification and arithmetic are absent from the notebook. This is not the same object as the preceding set-sized ultrapower.',
 fate=r'The field was published in \citet{conway1976numbers}, before this talk; \citet[Chs.~2--4]{gonshor1986surreal} systematically develops sign expansions. The readable modern verification is \citet[\S2.1--2.2]{mantovamatusinski2017survey}. The earlier occurrence 1978-446.22 concerns a different use of surreal/omnific arithmetic.',status='identified as classical surreal-number theory; the Kruskal label is retained as historical evidence.',refs=['conway1976numbers','gonshor1986surreal','mantovamatusinski2017survey'],pages='P001000',area='Surreal numbers and ordered fields')
add(543,'claim','The finite-arrow halving rule needs correction',
 r'The explanatory parenthesis says that the step is halved when the direction changes.',
 r'For a finite sign sequence beginning with \(k\ge1\) identical signs of value \(s\in\{1,-1\}\), its value is \(sk\) if it stops there. If the first change occurs after that initial run, each subsequent sign contributes successively \(\pm2^{-1},\pm2^{-2},\ldots\), irrespective of whether it changes direction again. Thus \(+-=1/2\), \(+-+=3/4\), and \(+--=1/4\). Halving only on each direction change would instead give the incorrect value zero for \(+--\).',
 r'The supplied three examples do not distinguish the two rules. A fourth example does, so the correction concerns a faithful mathematical shorthand rather than a transcription error.',
 fate=r'The sign-expansion construction is treated in \citet[Chs.~2--4]{gonshor1986surreal} and recalled in \citet[\S2.1]{mantovamatusinski2017survey}. The corrected finite rule follows from the simplest-number-between-neighbors recursion.',status='the literal operational rule is false; the corrected rule recovers all finite surreal sign expansions.',correct='false as written',refs=['gonshor1986surreal','mantovamatusinski2017survey'],pages='P001000',area='Surreal numbers and ordered fields')
add(543,'claim','Infinite arrow blocks and the distinction between omega and infinity',
 r'An infinitely repeated up arrow is called \(\infty\); appending a down arrow gives \(\infty-1\). An up block followed by a down block is called \(\tfrac12\infty\), their ratio is 2, a double hat gives \(\infty^2\), and one up followed by infinitely many downs gives \(\varepsilon\).',
 r'Interpreting each single infinite block as length \(\omega\), the identities are \(+^\omega=\omega\), \(+^\omega-=\omega-1\), \(+^\omega-^\omega=\omega/2\), and \(+-^\omega=\omega^{-1}\). In particular \(\omega/(\omega/2)=2\). The double-hat identity becomes correct if it means a constant positive sequence of length \(\omega^2\). Here \(\omega\) is a positive infinite surreal, not the extended-real endpoint \(+\infty\).',
 r'The ordinal lengths and arithmetic convention are essential reconstructions. A vague ``infinite repetition of an infinite repetition\textquotesingle\ is not a definition. The nearby ``no predecessor\textquotesingle\ can refer to ordinal \(\omega\), but every surreal \(x\) has \(x-1\) and there are no immediate neighbors in an ordered field.',
 fate=r'These are consequences of the classical surreal order and arithmetic described in \citet{conway1976numbers} and \citet[Chs.~4--5]{gonshor1986surreal}; \citet[\S2]{mantovamatusinski2017survey} supplies the modern sign and ordinal conventions. The exact infinite-block formulas were checked by the canonical-cut interpretation, rather than treated as ordinary real limits.',status='correct after assigning ordinal lengths and replacing the overloaded infinity notation.',refs=['conway1976numbers','gonshor1986surreal','mantovamatusinski2017survey'],pages='P001000',area='Surreal numbers and ordered fields')
save()
add(544,'construction','An ascending union plausibly identified with a filtered elliptic matrix algebra',
 r'After naming joint work with Brozovskii, Dzyaloshinskii and Krichever, the source writes only \(G=\bigcup_nG_n\), \(G_n\subset G_{n+1}\). No group law, algebra structure, or definition of \(G_n\) is given.',
 r'A close later construction is the algebra of matrix functions on a punctured elliptic curve whose entries near the puncture have prescribed exponential factors \(e^{(x_i-x_j)/z}\) times Laurent series with finitely many negative powers. Bounding the pole order after a local diagonal exponential gauge gives finite-dimensional pieces \(G_n\), with \(G_nG_m\subseteq G_{n+m}\). This supplies an algebra filtration, not a claim that each \(G_n\) is a group.',
 r'The identification is a reconstruction supported by the immediately following sigma formula and by Krichever\textquotesingle s later notation \(G(\Gamma,z)\). The handwritten letter \(G\) alone does not authorize calling the union a group. The collaborator is almost certainly S. A. Brazovskii; the notebook\textquotesingle s first vowel is faithfully ``o\textquotesingle.',
 cls='A',fate=r'\citet[\S1.3, (1.91)--(1.94)]{krichever1983elliptic} defines the matching matrix-function framework; the original Russian survey is dated 1983 and the inspected English translation 1985. The joint \citet{brazovskiidzyaloshinskiikrichever1982peierls}, submitted 9 February 1982, confirms the collaboration but is only a thematic match to this isolated fragment.',status='partially identified with an established filtered-algebra construction; no complete seminar theorem can be recovered.',correct='not determinable',match='partial',refs=['krichever1983elliptic','brazovskiidzyaloshinskiikrichever1982peierls'],confidence='medium',pages='P001000',area='Integrable systems and elliptic functions')
add(544,'construction','Sigma-product entries and cancellation of elliptic multipliers',
 r'The source gives matrix entries \(u^{ij}(z)=\prod_{k=1}^n\sigma(z-a_k^{ij})\,\sigma(z)^{-n}\exp(\zeta(z)(x_i-x_j))\), with \(\sum_ka_k^{ij}=x_i-x_j\), with \(\Gamma\) labelled as a torus.',
 r'Fix a lattice \(\Lambda\subset\mathbb C\), its Weierstrass sigma and zeta functions, and chosen complex lifts satisfying the stated sum exactly. For a period \(2\omega\), the sigma quotient acquires multiplier \(e^{-2\eta\sum_ka_k^{ij}}\), whereas the exponential acquires \(e^{2\eta(x_i-x_j)}\); they cancel. Thus the displayed entry is single valued on \((\mathbb C/\Lambda)\setminus\{0\}\). Near zero it is \(e^{(x_i-x_j)/z}\) times a meromorphic germ of pole order at most \(n\). If \(x_i\ne x_j\), the puncture is generally essential, not a meromorphic pole.',
 r'The lattice, choice of lifts, and local nature of the exponential gauge are supplied. The gauge-transformed entry need not itself be a global elliptic function. Scalar prefactors may be added in a general factorization, but are not written in the notebook.',
 cls='A',fate=r'The analytic framework and sigma construction in \citet[\S1.3, (1.91)--(1.94)]{krichever1983elliptic} give a partial publication match. The period-cancellation calculation verifies exactly what the surviving formula supports; no unrecorded Lax equation, Peierls Hamiltonian, or claimed completeness theorem is assigned to it.',status='the displayed quasi-periodicity cancellation is correct under the stated choices; the intended global theorem remains unspecified.',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',refs=['krichever1983elliptic'],confidence='medium',pages='P001000',area='Integrable systems and elliptic functions')
save()
